  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "An analysis of a panel of 626 Chinese provincial border counties from 2007 to 2020 demonstrates that improved cross-border high-speed rail (HSR) network connectivity reduces PM2.5 levels. Using a two-way fixed effects model, the study calculates that a one-standard-deviation rise in cross-border HSR degree, closeness, and betweenness lowers local PM2.5 concentrations by 0.72%, 0.75%, and 0.63%, respectively. The network integration is accompanied by stronger interregional trade links, reduced local government land allocations to polluting industries, and narrower pollution disparities between border counties and wealthier regions.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that expanding physical transport networks across administrative boundaries can directly mitigate transboundary pollution externalities in historically overlooked peripheral border zones."
    }
  },
